e, particularly the tension between the Catholic royal house and certain Arian-leaning ducal factions. His actions precipitated a significant civil conflict that tested the stability of the kingdom during a critical period of religious consolidation.

Initially the Duke of Trent and later Brescia, Alahis leveraged local discontent and Arian sentiment to mount his rebellions. His first uprising in 679 CE, though ultimately suppressed by King Perctarit, foreshadowed his later success. In 688 CE, he capitalized on Cunincpert's absence and a wave of popular unrest to seize Pavia, the Lombard capital, forcing Cunincpert into exile on the island of Comacina. Alahis then governed Lombardy, facing opposition primarily from Cunincpert's loyalists and the powerful Catholic Church, which saw his Arianism as a threat to the kingdom's religious unity.

Alahis's short-lived usurpation ultimately failed to establish a lasting Arian resurgence or a new ducal dynasty. His defeat and death at the Battle of Cornate in 689 CE at the hands of Cunincpert's forces marked a crucial turning point, effectively ending the most significant Arian challenge to the Catholic Lombard monarchy. This victory allowed Cunincpert to consolidate his power, strengthen the Catholic identity of the kingdom, and pave the way for a more unified Lombard state, albeit one still prone to internal ducal rivalries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-16T00:45:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29462103260", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "700 CE, Geographical region of Italy"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "700 CE, Geographical region of Italy"}], "newValue": ["The enduring religious divide between the Arian and Catholic factions within the Lombard Kingdom fostered an environment ripe for rebellion.", "The political ambitions of powerful regional dukes, like Alahis, who sought greater autonomy or even the throne from the central royal authority.", "King Cunincpert's temporary unpopularity or perceived weakness in 688 CE provided Alahis with an opportune moment to launch his coup."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-16T00:45:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29462103260", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "689 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "689 CE"}], "newValue": ["Alahis's successful rebellion led to the temporary overthrow of King Cunincpert and a period of civil war within the Lombard Kingdom (688-689 CE).", "His defeat 